  "Notes": "Single quarter-wave rutile beam-splitter film on glass\nSpecification: Quarter-wave TiO2 at 550 nm on glass ng = 1.51 for a high-reflectance beam divider.\nReported: Table p. 338: R = 44.5 %, T = 55.0 % at 550 nm on glass ng = 1.51. Text p. 338: physical thickness 505 A, optical thickness nt = 1370 A (= lambda/4 at 550 nm). Fig. 6: Rmax of a lambda/4 rutile film on glass decreases from 52.9 % at 400 nm to 41.4 % at 800 nm (Rmax at each wavelength, i.e. a different film per wavelength); measured values not corrected for back reflection.\nNotes: Rutile index taken as the constant n = 1370/505 = 2.713 at 550 nm (the film dispersion of Fig. 5, about 2.9 at 400 nm to 2.5 at 2000 nm, is only plotted). Physical thickness 50.5 nm corresponds to 1 QWOT at 550 nm with this n. Normal incidence assumed; glass treated as a semi-infinite substrate (no back surface).\nLayers from Table on p. 338 (row 'TiO2'); thickness 505 A and optical thickness 1370 A from text p. 338; Fig. 6 (p. 337); layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours R 43.5 % at 550 nm; paper R 44.5 % (measured, table p. 338).",
